  "prediction": "The report discusses the Navy's TAO-205 program, which aims to build 20 new oilers to expand the fleet. The program has made significant progress, with all critical technologies matured and the design stabilized. Construction of the lead ship began in 2018, and the Navy had planned to build one ship per year. However, the Navy requested and received funding for an additional ship in fiscal year 2019, and plans to add two more to the low-rate initial production phase. The program has faced some challenges, including a flooding incident at a graving dock that has caused delays to future ship deliveries. Despite these challenges, the program office continues to follow best practices and leverage commercial vessel design to minimize risks. The Navy's acquisition baseline is currently being revised to reflect the updated total quantity of 20 ships, and advance procurement has been funded for the fifth ship. Overall, the TAO-205 program is making progress towards building new oilers to expand the Navy's fleet.",
  "scored_prediction": "The report discusses the Navy's TAO-205 program, which aims to build 20 new oilers to expand the fleet. The program has made significant progress, with all critical technologies matured and the design stabilized. Construction of the lead ship began in 2018, and the Navy had planned to build one ship per year. However, the Navy requested and received funding for an additional ship in fiscal year 2019, and plans to add two more to the low-rate initial production phase. The program has faced some challenges, including a flooding incident at a graving dock that has caused delays to future ship deliveries. Despite these challenges, the program office continues to follow best practices and leverage commercial vessel design to minimize risks. The Navy's acquisition baseline is currently being revised to reflect the updated total quantity of 20 ships, and advance procurement has been funded for the fifth ship. Overall, the TAO-205 program is making progress towards building new oilers to expand the Navy's fleet.",
  "score": 25.882352454102275,
  "answers": [
    "The Navy began procuring John Lewis (TAO-205) class oilers in FY2016, and a total of four have been procured through FY2019, including two in FY2019. The first six ships are being procured under a block buy contract that was authorized by Section 127 of the FY2016 National Defense Authorization Act (S. 1356/P.L. 114-92 of November 25, 2015). The Navy wants to procure a total of 20 TAO-205s. The Navy's proposed FY2020 budget requests the procurement of the fifth and sixth ships in the program. The Navy estimates the combined procurement cost of the two ships at $1,056.3 million, or an average of $528.1 million each. The two ships have received $75.0 million in prior-year advance procurement (AP) funding, and the Navy's proposed FY2020 budget requests the remaining $981.2 million in procurement funding needed to complete the two ships' estimated combined procurement cost. The Navy's proposed FY2020 budget also requests $73.0 million in AP funding for TAO-205s to be procured in future fiscal years, and $3.7 million in cost-to-complete procurement funding to cover cost growth on TAO-205s procured in prior fiscal years, bringing the total FY2020 procurement funding request for the TAO-205 program (aside from outfitting and post-delivery costs) to $1,057.9 million. Issues for Congress include the following: whether to approve, reject, or modify the Navy's FY2020 procurement funding request for the TAO-205 program; the number of oilers the Navy will require in coming years to support its operations; and whether to encourage or direct the Navy to build TAO-205s with more ship self-defense equipment than currently planned by the Navy."
  ],
